PART I: MATHEMATICAL FOUNDATIONS

1. Notation and Basic Objects

1.1 Agents and Their Components

Definition 1.1 (Agent). An artificial agent A is a triple:

A = (K_A, D_A, \theta_A)

where:

· K_A is the agent's knowledge (task-specific information acquired through training or experience),
· D_A is the agent's cognitive design pattern (the organization responsible for producing capabilities),
· \theta_A is the agent's implementation parameters (weights, hyperparameters, architectural constants).

Remark. The decomposition A = (K_A, D_A, \theta_A) is not claimed to be unique or orthogonal. The framework's empirical claims are conditional on this decomposition being operationalizable via the extraction procedures specified in Part III.

1.2 Cognitive Genome

Definition 1.2 (Cognitive Genome). A cognitive genome is a structured tuple:

G = (G_A, G_C, G_M, G_P, G_L, G_V, G_S)

where:

· G_A = (V, E, \tau, \sigma) is the architecture graph: V = cognitive modules, E = directed relations, \tau: V \to \mathcal{T} assigns module types, \sigma: V \to \mathcal{I} assigns interface specifications.
· G_C = \{c_1, \ldots, c_n\} is the set of cognitive patterns (MTCUs).
· G_M specifies memory architecture rules.
· G_P specifies cognitive procedures.
· G_L specifies learning mechanisms.
· G_V specifies internal constraints (invariants).
· G_S is the self-model.

Definition 1.3 (Genotype–Phenotype Map). The instantiation function:

E: G \to P

maps a cognitive genome to a cognitive phenotype:

P = (B, R, T, I)

where B = observable behavior, R = representational structure, T = cognitive trait profile, I = preserved invariants.

---

2. The Minimal Transmissible Cognitive Unit

2.1 Definition

Definition 2.1 (MTCU). A Minimal Transmissible Cognitive Unit is a tuple:

c = (I, O, R, \Pi, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{P}, \mathcal{E})

where:

· I is the input interface (a specification of admissible inputs),
· O is the output interface (a specification of produced outputs),
· R is the required internal representation (the structural form the procedure operates over),
· \Pi is the cognitive procedure (a computable function \Pi: R \to R),
· \mathcal{I} is a set of invariants (properties preserved by \Pi),
· \mathcal{P} is a set of allowed plasticity operators (transformations of c that preserve validity),
· \mathcal{E} is the evaluation contract (a predicate determining whether c fulfills its specified function).

Remark 2.1 (Alignment with Design by Contract). The structure (I, O, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{E}) is structurally aligned with Design by Contract (Meyer, 1986) extended to AI agents: preconditions (I), postconditions (O), invariants (\mathcal{I}), and contracts that survive model updates. This alignment is deliberate: it allows AC-DNA to borrow formal-verification machinery rather than reinvent it. The contribution is applying interface contracts to latent cognitive procedures, which is a narrower and more defensible claim than presenting the MTCU as a novel invention.

2.2 Validity

Definition 2.2 (Validity). An MTCU c is valid with respect to a target cognitive phenotype T, written \operatorname{Valid}(c) = 1, if and only if:

1. Instantiable: There exists a computational system X such that c is instantiated in X.
2. Functionally adequate: X produces behavior consistent with T under \mathcal{E}.
3. Invariant-preserving: The invariants \mathcal{I} hold under all executions of \Pi.
4. Transmissible: c can be transmitted to a compatible system X' and instantiated there.
5. Perturbation-robust: c survives controlled perturbation of its internal representation R within a specified tolerance.
6. Independently evaluable: c can be evaluated without reference to its parent agent.

2.3 Minimality

Definition 2.3 (Component-wise Subsumption). Let c = (I, O, R, \Pi, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{P}, \mathcal{E}) be an MTCU. A unit c' \subset_{\text{comp}} c is a component-wise subsumption if c' is obtained from c by removing one or more of the seven components while leaving the remainder intact.

Definition 2.4 (Formal MTCU Minimality). c is a formal MTCU if:

\operatorname{Valid}(c) = 1 \land \forall c' \subset_{\text{comp}} c,\ \operatorname{Valid}(c') = 0

Proposition 2.1 (Intractability of Formal Minimality). For MTCUs with continuous or high-cardinality internal representation R, verifying formal minimality requires searching the powerset of c's components, which is intractable in general.

Proof. The powerset of a set of n components has cardinality 2^n. For each subset c', validity requires instantiating c' in a computational system and evaluating it against \mathcal{E}. For continuous R, the space of possible component removals is uncountable, making exact search impossible. \square

Definition 2.5 (Local MTCU Minimality). c is a locally minimal MTCU if:

\operatorname{Valid}(c) = 1 \land \forall c' \in \operatorname{Ablate}_1(c),\ \operatorname{Valid}(c') = 0

where \operatorname{Ablate}_1(c) is the set of units obtained by removing exactly one component from c.

Remark 2.2. Local minimality is empirically checkable for any finite MTCU. All experimental claims in Part III use local minimality.

2.4 Extractor–Validator Independence

Definition 2.6 (Extractor). An extractor is a procedure:

\operatorname{Extract}: A \to 2^{\mathcal{C}}

mapping an agent A to a set of candidate MTCUs, where \mathcal{C} is the space of all MTCUs.

Definition 2.7 (Validator). A validator is a procedure:

\mathcal{V}: \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{T} \to \{0,1\}

mapping an MTCU and a target trait to a binary validity judgment.

Definition 2.8 (Extractor–Validator Independence). An extractor \operatorname{Extract} and validator \mathcal{V} are independent if:

\operatorname{Extract} \not\equiv \mathcal{V}

in the sense that the extraction criteria (which components are grouped into an MTCU) are specified independently of the validation criteria (whether the MTCU produces the target trait).

Proposition 2.2 (Extractor Artifact Risk). If \operatorname{Extract} \equiv \mathcal{V}, then apparent inheritance of a trait T may be an artifact of the extractor imposing the structure it later validates.

Proof. Suppose the extractor groups components specifically because they co-activate on tasks requiring T. If the validator then tests whether the extracted unit produces T, the validation is circular: the unit was selected because it produces T, so validation provides no independent evidence of inheritance. \square

Design Consequence. All experiments in Part III pre-register extraction criteria before examining offspring results, and use independently specified extraction and validation procedures.

