3. Causal Heritability

3.1 The Frozen-Evaluation Framework

Definition 3.1 (Instantiation and Learning). Let G_X be a cognitive genome. Define:

X_0 = \operatorname{Instantiate}(G_X)

as the frozen descendant (pre-learning), and:

X_t = \operatorname{Learn}(X_0, G_L, t)

as the post-learning descendant after t steps of G_L-driven self-modification.

Remark 3.1. The distinction between X_0 and X_t is essential. Machine Zygote (Saoud, 2026) freezes the newborn before any learning to isolate germline effects from developmental noise. AC-DNA adopts this methodological logic while acknowledging that Saoud's measured traits are morphological and locomotory, not cognitive, and that the study is an unreviewed preprint. The frozen-evaluation checkpoint is methodological inspiration, not empirical support for cognitive-pattern inheritance.

3.2 Heritability Estimands

Definition 3.2 (Matched Germline Substitution). Let G_A, G_B be two parental genomes. Let \mathcal{R}(G_A, G_B, \xi) be a recombination operator with fixed randomness \xi. A matched germline substitution replaces exactly one parental germline while holding \xi, mutation vectors, and developmental-noise seeds fixed.

Definition 3.3 (Stage I Heritability). The germline heritability of trait T is:

H_0(T) = \mathbb{E}\left[ T(X_0) \mid do(G_X = \mathcal{R}(G_A, G_B)) \right] - \mathbb{E}\left[ T(X_0) \mid do(G_X = G_{\text{control}}) \right]

where G_{\text{control}} is a matched counterfactual genome preserving all stochastic background except parental germline identity.

Definition 3.4 (Stage II Heritability). The developmental persistence of trait T is:

H_t(T) = \mathbb{E}\left[ T(X_t) \mid do(G_X = \mathcal{R}(G_A, G_B)) \right] - \mathbb{E}\left[ T(X_t) \mid do(G_X = G_{\text{control}}) \right]

Definition 3.5 (Transgressive Offspring). Let [T_{\min}, T_{\max}] be the interval of trait values observed in parental clone lines. A descendant is transgressive if T(X_0) \notin [T_{\min}, T_{\max}]. The transgressive fraction is the proportion of offspring satisfying this condition.

Remark 3.2. The transgressive-offspring statistic from Machine Zygote (15.0% vs. 1.2%) illustrates the method of interval-based emergence detection. It is not evidence for cognitive-pattern emergence in AC-DNA's target domains.

3.3 The MI Measurability Constraint

Theorem 3.1 (McAllester–Stratos, 2020). Any distribution-free, high-confidence lower bound on mutual information estimated from N samples cannot be larger than O(\ln N).

Corollary 3.1 (Operational Consequence). If the true I(K_P; C) is large (hundreds of bits), no distribution-free method can confidently certify it is near zero.

Corollary 3.2 (Revised Operationalization). The MI-based criterion:

I(K_P; C) \approx 0

is retained as interpretive framing but is not the operational falsification gate. The operational test uses held-out-task performance under datasets-disjoint and shared-pretraining-controlled conditions. MI estimates are reported as scoped secondary measurements in the regime of small expected leakage.

Definition 3.6 (Operational Heritability Criterion). The primary AC-DNA claim is:

\boxed{ H_0(T) > 0 \quad \text{under matched germline substitution} }

with:

\boxed{ F_{\text{held-out}}(X_0) > F(\text{controls})_{\text{held-out}} \quad \text{under datasets-disjoint training} }

MI estimates \widehat{I}(K_P; G_X) and \widehat{I}(\theta_P; G_X) are reported using the difference-of-entropies estimator, which provides neither upper nor lower bound guarantees but can meaningfully estimate large mutual information from feasible samples.

