4. Recombination

4.1 Compatibility

Definition 4.1 (Compatibility Function). For two MTCUs c_i = (I_i, O_i, R_i, \Pi_i, \mathcal{I}_i, \mathcal{P}_i, \mathcal{E}_i) and c_j, the compatibility is:

\Gamma(c_i, c_j) = f\left(I_j, O_i, \mathcal{I}_i, \mathcal{I}_j, R_i, R_j, \operatorname{Sem}(R_i, R_j)\right) \in [0,1]

where \operatorname{Sem}(R_i, R_j) measures semantic alignment between internal representations.

Definition 4.2 (Compatibility Threshold). For a threshold \tau \in (0,1):

\Gamma(c_i, c_j) < \tau \Rightarrow \mathcal{R}(c_i, c_j) = \varnothing

4.2 Recombination Operator

Definition 4.3 (Recombination). Given two parent genomes G_A, G_B, the candidate offspring genome is:

G_X^* = \mathcal{R}(G_A, G_B, \xi)

where \xi specifies permitted transformation operators:

\mathcal{R} = \{\text{module selection}, \text{graph composition}, \text{procedure composition}, \text{interface adaptation}, \text{representation alignment}, \text{constraint intersection}\}

Proposition 4.1 (Compatibility Is Not Similarity). Two cognitive systems need not be similar to be compatible.

Proof. Consider c_A = \text{planner} and c_B = \text{verifier}. These may have low representational similarity \operatorname{Sem}(R_A, R_B) \approx 0 but high compatibility \Gamma(c_A, c_B) \approx 1 because O_A satisfies I_B and O_B satisfies I_A in a complementary pipeline. \square

