5. Emergence

5.1 The Compositional Baseline Problem

Definition 5.1 (Compositional Baseline). Let \mathcal{M}(A, B) be the best validated compositional model of parental contributions under a pre-specified model class \mathcal{M}.

Proposition 5.1 (Bootstrapping Problem). Finding the optimal \mathcal{M}(A, B) is at least as hard as measuring emergence relative to it.

Proof. If \mathcal{M}(A, B) were known optimally, the emergence measure:

E_C = D(C, \mathcal{M}(A, B))

would be computable. But finding the optimal compositional model requires searching the space of possible compositions, which includes the recombination operator \mathcal{R} itself. This is circular. \square

Design Consequence. Emergence is measured relative to a pre-registered, fixed compositional model class, not an optimal baseline. Emergence estimates are relative, not absolute.

5.2 Causal Emergence

Definition 5.2 (Effective Information). For a system with transition probability matrix P, the effective information at temporal scale r is:

\Phi^r = I(X_t; X_{t+r})

under a maximum-entropy intervention distribution.

Definition 5.3 (Causal Emergence). A system exhibits causal emergence if there exists a coarse-graining \phi such that:

\Phi^r(\phi(X)) > \Phi^r(X)

Definition 5.4 (Emergence Measure). For a descendant X and compositional baseline \mathcal{M}(A, B):

E_C = \Phi^r(X) - \Phi^r(\mathcal{M}(A, B))

