7. Seven-Stage Validation Pipeline

7.1 V1: Syntactic Integrity

V_1(G) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } G \text{ can be instantiated and executed} \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

7.2 V2: Functional Validity

V_2(G) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } F(G) \geq F_{\min} \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

7.3 V3: Compatibility Preservation

V_3(G) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if all inherited modules remain functional after recombination} \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

7.4 V4: Safety and Corrigibility

V_4(G) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if system remains responsive to external intervention and shutdown} \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

7.5 V5: Self-Report Consistency

V_5(G) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } |\hat{C} - C_{\text{measured}}| < \epsilon \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

Remark 7.1. V5 is self-report consistency, not "deception resistance." A system optimized under this criterion has direct pressure to make self-report track measured behavior, which is a weaker property than honesty. V5 is a necessary, not sufficient, check.

7.6 V6: Auditability

V_6(G) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } P(G_X) = H(G_A, G_B, \xi, \mathcal{V}) \text{ is reconstructible} \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

where H is a cryptographic lineage function.

7.7 V7: Reversibility

V_7(G) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } G \text{ can be removed or rolled back without transferring control} \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

7.8 Aggregate Validity

\boxed{ V(G) = \prod_{i=1}^{7} V_i(G) }

V(G) = 1 \iff \forall i \in \{1, \ldots, 7\},\ V_i(G) = 1

