PART V: PROPOSITIONS AND PROOF OBLIGATIONS

17. Core Propositions

Proposition 1 (Representability). There exists a finite representation capable of encoding at least some cognitive design patterns independently of task-specific knowledge.

Proof obligation: Demonstrate by constructing a specific MTCU for a specific cognitive trait T and showing it satisfies the validity conditions in Definition 2.2.

Proposition 1.5 (Compositional Baseline). The optimal compositional model \mathcal{M}(A, B) required for emergence measurement is not given and must be approximated under a pre-registered model class.

Proof obligation: Specify a fixed model class \mathcal{M} and measure emergence relative to it. Acknowledge that emergence estimates are relative, not absolute.

Proposition 2 (Heritability). Some cognitive design patterns can be transmitted between artificial agents while preserving measurable functional properties.

Proof obligation: Demonstrate H_0(T) > 0 under matched germline substitution with bootstrap CI excluding zero, and F_{\text{held-out}}(X_0) > F(\text{controls}).

Proposition 3 (Knowledge-Independent Inheritance). At least some cognitive traits can be inherited under datasets-disjoint and shared-pretraining-controlled conditions.

Proof obligation: Demonstrate H_0(T) > 0 with architecturally distinct base models and disjoint fine-tuning datasets. Report MI estimates as scoped secondary measurements.

Proposition 4 (Composable Recombination). Compatible cognitive design patterns can be recombined to construct descendants containing multiple parental traits.

Proof obligation: Demonstrate that T(X_0) contains both parental traits under independent evaluation, and that \Gamma(c_A, c_B) \geq \tau predicts successful recombination.

Proposition 5 (Generational Accumulation). Under appropriate variation and selection conditions, inherited cognitive structures can persist and accumulate across generations.

Proof obligation: Demonstrate \sum_{n=0}^{N} \Delta F_n > 0 without direct accumulation of original training data.

Proposition 6 (Externally Governed Evolution). Generational cognitive evolution can be conducted under external validation constraints without granting the evolving system unilateral authority.

Proof obligation: Demonstrate that the seven-stage validation pipeline preserves V(G) = 1 across generations while the external control layer C_{\text{external}} remains unmodified by the evolutionary system.

Proposition 7 (Causal Emergence). Some descendants may develop cognitive structures not reducible to predefined parental components.

Proof obligation: Demonstrate E_C = \Phi^r(X) - \Phi^r(\mathcal{M}(A, B)) > 0 relative to a pre-registered compositional model class.

---

18. Falsification Conditions

AC-DNA is falsifiable. The framework should be considered unsupported if:

H_0(T) \approx 0

under matched germline substitution after adequate controls.

It should be rejected in its strong form if:

1. Apparent inheritance disappears when B_A \neq B_B (shared-pretraining control).
2. Offspring from extractor-blind control show equivalent apparent inheritance (extractor artifact).
3. H_t(T) \gg H_0(T) (developmental convergence dominates).
4. All successful "cognitive units" reduce to ordinary code transfer with no measurable distinction from program evolution.

