A.2 Categorical Framework for Cognitive Composition

A.2.1 The Category \mathbf{Cog}

Definition A.2.1. The category \mathbf{Cog} has:

· Objects: MTCUs c = (I, O, R, \Pi, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{P}, \mathcal{E})
· Morphisms: Compatible transformations f: c_i \to c_j such that:
  · O_i \subseteq I_j (output of c_i satisfies input of c_j)
  · \mathcal{I}_i \cap \mathcal{I}_j \neq \varnothing (invariants are compatible)

Proposition A.2.1. \mathbf{Cog} is a category with composition inherited from function composition.

Proof.

· Identity: For any MTCU c, the identity morphism \operatorname{id}_c: c \to c exists since O \subseteq I trivially holds under interface reflection and \mathcal{I} \cap \mathcal{I} = \mathcal{I} \neq \varnothing.
· Composition: Given f: c_A \to c_B and g: c_B \to c_C, define g \circ f: c_A \to c_C. We have O_A \subseteq I_B \subseteq O_B \subseteq I_C, so O_A \subseteq I_C. Also (\mathcal{I}_A \cap \mathcal{I}_B) \cap (\mathcal{I}_B \cap \mathcal{I}_C) = \mathcal{I}_A \cap \mathcal{I}_B \cap \mathcal{I}_C \neq \varnothing.
· Associativity: Inherited from function composition. \square

A.2.2 Functorial Genotype–Phenotype Map

Definition A.2.2. The instantiation functor E: \mathbf{Gen} \to \mathbf{Phen} maps:

· Genomes G to phenotypes P = E(G)
· Recombination operators \mathcal{R}: G_A \times G_B \to G_X to phenotype-level operations E(\mathcal{R}): P_A \times P_B \to P_X

Proposition A.2.2. If E is a functor, then E(\mathcal{R}(G_A, G_B)) = E(\mathcal{R})(E(G_A), E(G_B)).

Proof. Follows directly from functoriality. \square

Remark A.2.1. Whether E is genuinely functorial is an empirical question. Non-functoriality would manifest as context-dependent instantiation, where the same genome produces different phenotypes depending on the instantiation environment.

A.3 Partial Order on MTCU Components

Definition A.3.1 (Component Lattice). For an MTCU c = (I, O, R, \Pi, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{P}, \mathcal{E}), the component lattice \mathcal{L}(c) is the powerset 2^{\{I,O,R,\Pi,\mathcal{I},\mathcal{P},\mathcal{E}\}} ordered by inclusion.

Definition A.3.2 (Validity Monotonicity). Validity is monotone if for all c' \subseteq c:

\operatorname{Valid}(c) = 1 \Rightarrow \operatorname{Valid}(c') \leq \operatorname{Valid}(c)

Proposition A.3.1. If validity is monotone, then the set of valid sub-units forms a down-closed subset of \mathcal{L}(c).

Proof. If c is valid and c' \subseteq c, monotonicity gives \operatorname{Valid}(c') \leq 1. In the binary case, \operatorname{Valid}(c') = 1 or 0, so the valid subsets are precisely those closed under taking subsets. \square

Remark A.3.2. In practice, validity is not monotone: removing a component may destroy validity. Local minimality (Definition 2.5) characterizes the boundary of the valid region.

A.4 Effective Information and Causal Emergence

A.4.1 Formal Definition

Definition A.4.1 (Effective Information). For a system with state space \mathcal{S}, transition probability matrix P, and intervention distribution \mathcal{D}_{\text{int}}, the effective information at temporal scale r is:

\Phi^r = I(X_t; X_{t+r})

where X_t \sim \mathcal{D}_{\text{int}} is a maximum-entropy (uniform) distribution over \mathcal{S}.

A.4.2 Coarse-Graining

Definition A.4.2 (Coarse-Graining). A coarse-graining is a surjective map \phi: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{S}' with |\mathcal{S}'| < |\mathcal{S}|.

Definition A.4.3 (Causal Emergence). A system exhibits causal emergence if:

\exists \phi: \Phi^r(\phi(X)) > \Phi^r(X)

Proposition A.4.1 (Hoel et al., 2013). Causal emergence occurs if and only if the coarse-graining \phi identifies causally redundant micro-states.

Proof sketch. If \phi groups micro-states that have identical transition probabilities to all macro-states, then the macro-transition matrix P' has higher determinism than P, increasing effective information. Conversely, if \Phi^r(\phi(X)) > \Phi^r(X), the coarse-graining must have eliminated noise, which requires redundant micro-states. \square

