APPENDIX D: FORMAL VERIFICATION MACHINERY

D.1 Design by Contract for MTCUs

D.1.1 Contract Specification Language

Definition D.1.1 (Contract). A contract \mathcal{K} for an MTCU c is a triple:

\mathcal{K} = (\operatorname{Pre}, \operatorname{Post}, \operatorname{Inv})

where:

· \operatorname{Pre}: I \to \{\text{true}, \text{false}\} is the precondition,
· \operatorname{Post}: I \times O \to \{\text{true}, \text{false}\} is the postcondition,
· \operatorname{Inv}: R \to \{\text{true}, \text{false}\} is the invariant.

Definition D.1.2 (Contract Satisfaction). An MTCU c satisfies contract \mathcal{K}, written c \models \mathcal{K}, if:

\forall i \in I: \operatorname{Pre}(i) \Rightarrow \operatorname{Post}(i, \Pi(i)) \land \operatorname{Inv}(R)

D.1.2 Contract Composition

Proposition D.1.1 (Contract Inheritance). If c_A \models \mathcal{K}_A and c_B \models \mathcal{K}_B and c_X = \mathcal{R}(c_A, c_B), then c_X \models \mathcal{K}_A \cap \mathcal{K}_B.

Proof. By definition of recombination, c_X inherits the components of both c_A and c_B. For any input i satisfying both preconditions, the postconditions of both are satisfied, and the invariants of both hold. Therefore c_X satisfies the intersected contract. \square

Proposition D.1.2 (Contract Weakening). If c \models \mathcal{K} and \mathcal{K}' \subseteq \mathcal{K} (i.e., \mathcal{K}' is weaker), then c \models \mathcal{K}'.

Proof. A weaker contract has fewer requirements. Any execution satisfying \mathcal{K} automatically satisfies \mathcal{K}'. \square

D.1.3 Runtime Verification

```
RuntimeVerification(c, input):
  1: // Check precondition
  2: if not c.E.Pre(input) then
  3:     return CONTRACT_VIOLATION_PRE
  4: end if
  5:
  6: // Execute procedure
  7: output ← c.Π(input)
  8:
  9: // Check postcondition
 10: if not c.E.Post(input, output) then
 11:     return CONTRACT_VIOLATION_POST
 12: end if
 13:
 14: // Check invariant
 15: if not c.I_inv(c.R) then
 16:     return CONTRACT_VIOLATION_INV
 17: end if
 18:
 19: return SUCCESS
```

